with the previous slide these are again  the most interesting dreams from 150  samples  um but you should think to yourself you  know what do you want to train  your program synthesizer on do you want  to train it on data that looks like this  or do you want to train it on data that  looks like this so once it's  learned its library um that kind of  bootstraps the training data for the  neural network  so we can also train it not to draw  pictures by moving a pen over a canvas  but also to  build towers by moving a hand in a kind  of tetris-like world  so here we give it these construction  problems um  and if you look at this learn library  you see uh  kind of like higher order uh like  planning macros so these are kind of  like options  but they're parametric  and again we can look at the dreams  before learning  and we can look at the dreams after  learning and again what you see is that  learning this library gives better  training data for the neural network  so it's also helpful to look at the  system's learning trajectory over  successive weight sleep cycles so at the  initial point of learning  almost no problems are solvable only the  easiest problems can be solved  but then over successive like sleep  cycles the system is able to solve more  problems  learn a better library trainer a better  neural network  use that to solve more problems and so  on and so you get this kind of boost  dropping action  um now you might be thinking do you  really need both of these uh sleep  phases  but if you ablate either of them  interestingly you still get the same  bootstrapping action you just get  much lower asymptotic performance you  can also compare with a range of bass  lines  so i think that the most interesting  baseline here is the enumeration  baseline  which asks what would happen if you did  a huge amount  of search without any learning  whatsoever so in particular what if you  just ran  an enumerative synthesizer for 24 hours  per problem  here we are running it for 10 minutes  per problem and what you see is that  even with  more search uh like you still need  learning in order to get the ball  rolling  and if you look across many different  domains um the same kind of global  picture emerges  that uh learning unfolds in a kind of  bootstrapping way  over multiple wake sleep cycles and that  you really do need  both of these sleep phases in order to  get to the highest level of asymptotic  performance  so i think that the  reason why each of these uh sleep phases  does better why you need both of them  and not just one of them  um can be explained very intuitively um  and it's by  thinking about these three phases of the  algorithm as follows  so during waking we're kind of doing  like problem solving or synthesis  and as we solve more problems as we  successfully synthesize more  uh solutions we then have more training  data for the library  but as you saw in the pre and post  learning examples for the dreams  a better library gives better training  data  for the recognition model for the neural  network  but the recognition model is being used  to  guide the combinatorial search over all  programs  so a better recognition model means that  you can solve more problems during  waking  so you get this kind of virtuous cycle  where when one part of the system  improves it kind of echoes and  reverberates  throughout the rest of the system and  that uh and that you know  sort of causes them to interact  synergistically um  so this story is i think fairly  intuitive and there's also some  empirical data  to back it up that empirical data comes  from looking at the evolution of the  library structure  over the system's learning trajectory so  here what i'm plotting  on the x-axis is a statistic of the  library structure  namely its depth when viewed as a  network  and on the y-axis i'm plotting how many  problems the system can solve  when it has that library um  in darker shade i'm showing what's going  on early in training and in  brighter shade i'm showing what's going  on later in learning so as the system  learns more as it goes through more of  these cycles the libraries get deeper  and also the libraries tend to solve  more problems  now here's what happens if you ablate  the  neural network so if you get rid of the  dream phase of sleep  so as you can see uh two things happen  one is that the neural networks be sorry  the libraries become more shallow  and also for a given library depth  you still solve fewer problems so  by having the neural network we both get  better libraries  because we get deeper libraries and  those are correlated with performance  but also for a given library depth  there's an extra bump to performance  from the neural network so adding the  neural network  improves the libraries that you get and  analogous but weaker trends fold if you  look at a different statistic  of the library structure in particular  it's uh size  um so in what i've talked about so far  um we built in a uh  what was still like a a very  customisable language you know like a  language for local graphics  or a language for moving a hand around  in a tetris-like world  but in principle you know we should be  able to start with a more generic kind  of programming language  and then use the exact same algorithm in  order to enrich it and move it closer to  something like a  domain specific language so  i'm going to look at two case studies  which explore this one which starts with  something like a modern functional  programming language  and then learns a language for vector  algebra and deserts  and then one which starts with a very  basic kind of lisp and then tries to  learn something which looks like that  modern functional language  so for physics what we did is we took  equations from ap physics and mcat cheat  sheets  and we simulated random data so we took  each equation  and ran it with random input outputs and  then  um got kind of like simulated data for  each equation  