 in  functional programming primitives  we wanted to learn them from a  more generic and smaller basis um so  particularly uh we give it things like  cons car and quitter  and also crucially recursion via the y  combinator  and we gave it like a problem set of  around two dozen  or so uh functional programming  exercises  um so interestingly in the very first  layer of its learned library  the thing that it defines the things  that defined in its like first  sleep cycle where these two functions  one of them is called fold which  recursively consumes a list  and one of them is called unfold which  recursively generates a list  so each of these can be defined in terms  of the y combinator  and um in a very precise technical sense  um they kind of are like a basis set  that spans the ways that you could  operate recursively over lists and once  you have fold and unfold you  don't actually need the y combinator in  order to do  uh recursions over lists um and  so it turns out that the system decides  to never use the y combinator again  besides that's like too cumbersome and  instead to  build up routines on top of map and fold  so familiar things  like map and filter which are folds um  and these sort of unfamiliar unfolds  that it decides to define  things like this routine i called count  two and countdown and unfold list  but also familiar unfolds like uh if you  know range from python that's an example  of unfold  in addition to these kind of  unconventional unfolds that decided to  define this range routine  as well as things that bring together  the full and unfold families like  indexing into a list  so the style of coding where instead of  doing explicit recursion  you use these higher level operators  well then unfold is sometimes called  origami programming  and i really like this um uh acquisition  here by  jeremy gibbons um although i the idea  itself is uh older  um than this citation here um  and while it's interesting that it kind  of um  retraced this 