ng about  synthesis is that it makes it very  convenient to build in the kinds of  things that  we know our good ideas and then learn on  top  of uh those things that we  we built in um so  that's it and i'd like to thank the  organizers and my collaborators  and i'll take questions thank you kevin  for the great talk  uh please i would like to encourage  attendees to ask questions it seems like  the radu is ready  yeah so very nice very inspiring talk  kevin so  so the main idea is of the library is  that uh  you have the maximum amount of sharing  uh among the codes  yeah so so you can think of it as  actually the set of capabilities  uh let's say uh of an agent  so and you can grow now one one  interesting question is how  you know in order to to get to more  tasks and more things  you have to give them more data input  data am i right  so how do you come up with that so  you know yeah yeah so i think that  what you want is something which covers  like a  wide range of difficulty levels and  something which also  uh makes up a consistent domain so for  example  if you had like very easy graph search  problems  and very difficult like image generation  problems  um that clearly wouldn't work um so  uh for example one thing like like one  of our domains  which i didn't discuss in the talk was  this uh generative regular expression  domain  where we crawled a bunch of csv files  from the web  and this kind of naturally had like  a spectrum of difficulty because some  csv files  are very simple and small like maybe  they're just  like monetary amounts and others are  more complicated like it might be like  addresses  and so the tasks here were to generate  um each of these csv columns um and so  you end up getting like a nice  spectrum of difficulty uh and also you  get  like a consistent domain because you  tend to see like reuse of concepts  across csv columns you know like uh  you might have like one column which is  just years and like another column  which is li